<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Cucaracha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">origins obscure; mentioned in an <a href="/years/1819">1819</a> Mexican novel; verses from the Hispano-Moroccan War (<a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a>); most known verses from the Mexican Revolution (<a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–c. 1920)</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;La Cucaracha&quot; (&quot;The Cockroach&quot;) is a folk song whose refrain tells of a cockroach that has lost two of its legs and can no longer walk properly. Its origins are obscure and Spanish. Because the refrain names no event, it cannot be dated, but its verses were made up anew for each time and place and so can be roughly dated by the events they mention.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>&quot;La Cucaracha&quot;, &quot;The Cockroach&quot;, is a popular folk song about a cockroach that cannot walk. Its origins are Spanish and obscure. The refrain makes no explicit reference to historical events and so is difficult, if not impossible, to date, but the verses were improvised to the needs of the time and mention contemporary social or political conditions, so a rough age can be estimated for them. In his book Cantos Populares Españoles of 1883, Francisco Rodríguez Marín noted lyrics about the Hispano-Moroccan War of 1859-1860, probably developed by the troops during the campaign to boost their morale, using an existing melody.</p><p>One of the earliest written references to the song is in the 1819 novel La Quijotita y su Prima by the Mexican writer José Joaquín Fernández de Lizardi. Other early stanzas concern the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlist-wars">Carlist Wars</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-intervention-in-mexico">French intervention in Mexico</a> in 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song consists of verse-and-refrain pairs, each half of four lines with an ABCB rhyme scheme. The earliest lyrics, from which its name derives, concern a cockroach that has lost two of its six legs and struggles to walk on the remaining four. The refrain of most later versions rests on them. The verses fit a traditional melody separate from that of the refrain, and they are highly variable, usually giving satirical commentary on contemporary political or social problems or disputes. Many later versions extend the last syllable of each line to fit a 6/4 meter, while almost all modern versions use 4/4 with a clave rhythm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, from 1910 to about 1920, saw the first major period of verse production for the song, because both rebel and government forces invented political lyrics for it, and most stanzas known today date from then. The two most quoted are Villista stanzas against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a>, in which the cockroach is said to represent the president, whom the Villistas considered a villain and traitor for his part in the death of President Francisco Madero. Competing Huertista, Zapatista and non-aligned versions also existed, some complaining of the hardships of the war, such as the high price of milk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>.</p><p>Soldiers in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a> sang of a soldadera, a woman who followed the troops, who wanted money to go to the bullfights. Among the Villistas, according to Elizabeth Salas, the cockroach wanted money for alcohol and marijuana. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-orwell">George Orwell</a>'s novel Animal Farm of 1945, the animals' rebellion song &quot;Beasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>&quot; is described as a blend of the tunes of &quot;La Cucaracha&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a>&quot;.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Cucaracha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>La Cucaracha</b>&quot; (&quot;The Cockroach&quot;) is a folk song whose refrain tells of a cockroach that has lost two of its legs and can no longer walk properly.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-cucaracha">La Cucaracha</a></li><li><blockquote>…was the theme of the North Korean film The Flower Girl (1972), whose music was claimed to be the work of Kim Il-sung, and in George Orwell's Animal Farm (1945) the tune of &quot;Beasts of England&quot; is described as something between &quot;<b>La Cucaracha</b>&quot; and &quot;Oh My Darling, Clementine&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a></li></ul></section>
